faq:
  - q: "Is 30 too late to change careers?"
    a: "No. Your 30s are among the most valuable career pivots you can make — you have enough experience to be credible in a new field and enough runway to build expertise before your 40s. The most effective career changes in your 30s transfer existing skills to a higher-leverage role (engineer to product manager, analyst to strategist, IC to manager) rather than starting completely from scratch. With the right positioning, a career change in your 30s typically takes 6–18 months to complete — not years."
  - q: "What should I focus on in my career in my 30s?"
    a: "Three priorities: (1) Compensation growth — your 30s are the decade where salary gaps compound the most. If you are below market, the time to close the gap is now. (2) Level-up to management or staff/principal IC — the transition from senior IC to manager or staff-level happens in your late 20s to mid-30s for most professionals. If you want this, you need to be actively pursuing it. (3) Network building — your 30s network becomes the foundation of everything in your 40s. The relationships you build now determine your access to senior roles later."
  - q: "How do I know if I need a career coach at 30?"
    a: "Consider a career coach if: you have been in the same role or at the same level for 2+ years without a clear path forward; you want to transition to management but do not know how to position for it; you have been underpaid relative to market and are not sure how to close the gap; you are considering a career change and need to understand how to transfer your skills; or you are job searching and not getting the results your experience should produce."
  - q: "How do I transition from individual contributor to manager in my 30s?"
    a: "Four moves: (1) Signal interest explicitly to your current manager — most managers do not proactively identify management candidates without a direct conversation. (2) Take on management-adjacent responsibilities now — mentoring, leading projects, onboarding new hires. These become the evidence in the 'why you' case. (3) Articulate a people leadership philosophy in interviews — not 'I want to manage people' but a specific view on how you develop teams and drive outcomes through others. (4) If your current company cannot promote you, target companies that are growing rapidly — they create management openings faster."
  - q: "What does career coaching for professionals in their 30s focus on?"
    a: "Depends on what is holding you back. Common focus areas: compensation gap analysis and salary negotiation strategy; management transition positioning; career change feasibility and skill transfer mapping; interview coaching for senior IC or manager-level roles; job search strategy for professionals who have been 'too busy to search' and need to move quickly."
